(A greeter. It reads its own command line and environment through the 's
 property syntax, then asks for your name and answers you.)

Print "=== The Command Line ===".

a number called 'argument count' is arguments's count.
Print "Arguments, counting the program's own name: {argument count}".

a text called program is arguments's name.
Print "This program was started as {program}".

(Greet whoever is named first on the command line, or else the world)
a text called name is "World".
If arguments's count is greater than 1 then,
    the name is arguments's first.
Print "Hello, {name}!".

If arguments's empty then,
    Print "(Nothing was given after the program's name.)".

Print "".
Print "=== The Environment ===".

a number called 'variable count' is environment's count.
Print "Environment variables: {variable count}".

(An environment variable is read by its name)
a text called user is environment's "USER".
Print "You are logged in as {user}.".

a text called home is environment's "HOME".
Print "Your home directory is {home}.".

a text called 'first variable' is environment's first.
Print "The first entry in the environment is {first variable}.".

Print "".
Print "=== A Conversation ===".

open a file for writing called screen at "/dev/stdout".
open a file for reading called keyboard at "/dev/stdin".
a buffer called answer is 256 bytes in size.
print "What's your name?".

(A line read from the keyboard keeps the newline typed after it, so the
 answer ends the greeting's line by itself)
read line from keyboard into answer.
a text called 'your name' is answer.
write "Nice to meet you, {your name}" to screen.
